{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-7595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975339/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b204d2003d34d114e6b00ab8994a740fce9daab25075e36eb96280815bfeea7"} {"question" "q-7595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be  $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975339/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b204d2003d34d114e6b00ab8994a740fce9daab25075e36eb96280815bfeea7"} {"question" "q-7595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975339/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e07a463e694d4763cb5ec6ca24900dc355da4f72a7817ba6541e964ea0d743d9"} {"question" "q-7595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975339/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b204d2003d34d114e6b00ab8994a740fce9daab25075e36eb9628081